1161. Maximum Level Sum of a Binary Tree

Problem
Given the root of a binary tree, level 1 contains the root, level 2 contains its children, etc.
Return the smallest level x such that the sum of all node values at level x is maximum.

Approach (BFS / Level-Order Traversal)
The most natural way to compute sums per level is Breadth-First Search (BFS):
	1.	Push the root into a queue.
	2.	While the queue is not empty:
	•	The current queue size equals the number of nodes on this level.
	•	Pop exactly that many nodes, add their values to level_sum.
	•	Push their children into the queue for the next level.
	3.	Track the best answer:
	•	best_sum = maximum level sum found so far
	•	best_level = earliest level that achieved best_sum
	4.	Update only when level_sum > best_sum (not >=) to keep the smallest level in case of ties.


Example
For root = [1,7,0,7,-8,null,null]:
	•	Level 1 sum = 1
	•	Level 2 sum = 7 + 0 = 7  ✅ (maximum)
	•	Level 3 sum = 7 + (-8) = -1
Answer: 2

Complexity
	•	Time: O(n) — each node is processed once
	•	Space: O(w) — w is the maximum width of the tree (max nodes in the queue at any level)